.TH "ELEMENT.SCROLLTO" "3JS" "July 9, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.scrollTo \- Element: scrollTo() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]scrollTo()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]Element\f[R]
interface scrolls to a particular set of coordinates inside a given
element.
.PP
This method is an alias for \f[CR]Element.scroll()\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
scrollTo(xCoord, yCoord)
scrollTo(options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]xCoord\f[R]
The x\-coordinate of the element\(cqs scrollable content that you want
the left edge of the element\(cqs scrollport to scroll to.
.TP
\f[B]yCoord\f[R]
The y\-coordinate of the element\(cqs scrollable content that you want
the top edge of the element\(cqs scrollport to scroll to.
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R]
An object containing the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]top\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
The y\-coordinate of the element\(cqs scrollable content that you want
the top edge of the element\(cqs scrollport to scroll to.
This is the same as the \f[CR]yCoord\f[R] parameter.
.TP
\f[B]left\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
The x\-coordinate of the element\(cqs scrollable content that you want
the left edge of the element\(cqs scrollport to scroll to.
This is the same as the \f[CR]xCoord\f[R] parameter.
.TP
\f[B]behavior\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
Determines whether scrolling is instant or animates smoothly.
This option is a string that must take one of the following values:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]smooth\f[R]: The scrolling animates smoothly.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]instant\f[R]: The scrolling happens instantly in a single jump.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]auto\f[R]: The scroll behavior is determined by the computed value
of the \f[CR]scroll\-behavior\f[R] CSS property on the element.
If omitted, \f[CR]behavior\f[R] defaults to \f[CR]auto\f[R].
.RE
.RE
.SS Return value
A \f[CR]Promise\f[R] that fulfills with an object containing the
following property:
.TP
\f[B]interrupted\f[R]
A boolean value indicating whether the scrolling operation was
interrupted (\f[CR]true\f[R]) or not (\f[CR]false\f[R]).
Such an interruption typically happens when a programmatic scroll is
ongoing, and another programmatic scroll is initiated on the same
element before the first one finishes.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
.IP
.EX
element.scrollTo(0, 1000);
.EE
.PP
Using \f[CR]options\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
element.scrollTo({
  top: 100,
  left: 100,
  behavior: \(dqsmooth\(dq,
});
.EE
.SS Responding to the end of the scroll
Our \c
.UR https://mdn.github.io/dom-examples/scroll-promises/element-methods/
element methods demo
.UE \c
\ (\c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/dom-examples/tree/main/scroll-promises/element-methods
see source code
.UE \c
) demonstrates how the promise return value of \f[CR]scrollTo()\f[R] can
be used to respond to the end of a scrolling operation.
This technique is mostly useful in cases where the scrolling occurs
smoothly over time (achieved by setting the \f[CR]behavior\f[R] option
to \f[CR]smooth\f[R], or by setting the scrolling element\(cqs
\f[CR]scroll\-behavior\f[R] property to \f[CR]smooth\f[R]).
.SS HTML
Our HTML includes a \f[CR]<section>\f[R] element containing several
paragraphs of content and a \f[CR]<div>\f[R] element toolbar containing
\f[CR]<button>\f[R] elements that trigger various scrolling operations
on the \f[CR]<section>\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R]>
  <\f[B]button\f[R] class=\(dqscroll\(dq>scroll() to 1000</\f[B]button\f[R]>
  <\f[B]button\f[R] class=\(dqscroll\-to\(dq>scrollTo() top</\f[B]button\f[R]>
  <\f[B]button\f[R] class=\(dqscroll\-by\(dq>scrollBy() 200</\f[B]button\f[R]>
  <\f[B]button\f[R] class=\(dqscroll\-into\-view\(dq>Scroll last &lt;p&gt; into view</\f[B]button\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>

<\f[B]section\f[R]>...</\f[B]section\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
We give the \f[CR]<section>\f[R] element a fixed \f[CR]height\f[R] and
an \f[CR]overflow\-y\f[R] value of \f[CR]scroll\f[R] so that it scrolls
vertically, and set its \f[CR]scroll\-behavior\f[R] property to
\f[CR]smooth\f[R] so that any scroll operations are animated smoothly
over time rather than instantly.
.IP
.EX
section {
  \f[B]border\f[R]: 1px solid black;
  \f[B]padding\f[R]: 20px;
  \f[B]margin\-top\f[R]: 60px;
  \f[B]height\f[R]: 500px;
  \f[B]overflow\-y\f[R]: scroll;
  \f[B]scroll\-behavior\f[R]: smooth;
}
.EE
.PP
We also create two class selectors; when a \f[CR]fade\-out\f[R] or
\f[CR]fade\-in\f[R] class is applied to an element, an
\f[CR]animation\f[R] is applied so that it smoothly fades out or in,
respectively.
We also define \f[CR]\(atkeyframes\f[R] blocks to define the required
\f[CR]opacity\f[R] changes for those animations.
.IP
.EX
\&.fade\-out {
  \f[B]animation\f[R]: fade\-out 0.3s linear both;
}

\&.fade\-in {
  \f[B]animation\f[R]: fade\-in 0.3s linear both;
}

\f[B]\(atkeyframes\f[R] fade\-out {
  from {
    \f[B]opacity\f[R]: 1;
  }

  to {
    \f[B]opacity\f[R]: 0;
  }
}

\f[B]\(atkeyframes\f[R] fade\-in {
  from {
    \f[B]opacity\f[R]: 0;
  }

  to {
    \f[B]opacity\f[R]: 1;
  }
}
.EE
.PP
The rest of the CSS is not shown, for brevity.
.SS JavaScript
We start by grabbing references to the \f[CR]<button>\f[R] that runs the
\f[CR]scrollTo()\f[R] operation, the toolbar \f[CR]<div>\f[R], and the
scrolling \f[CR]<section>\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] scrollToBtn = document.querySelector(\(dq.scroll\-to\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] toolbar = document.querySelector(\(dqdiv\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] section = document.querySelector(\(dqsection\(dq);
.EE
.PP
Next, we define a function called \f[CR]isInterrupted()\f[R], designed
to run in response to a scroll operation finishing, which takes a
boolean \f[CR]interrupted\f[R] value as a parameter.
It logs a message to the console to say that scrolling is finished and
indicate whether the operation was interrupted (\f[CR]interrupted\f[R]
is \f[CR]true\f[R]) or not.
In addition, if \f[CR]interrupted\f[R] is \f[CR]true\f[R], it calls an
\f[CR]alert()\f[R] to clearly indicate the interruption.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]function\f[R] isInterrupted(interrupted) {
  console.log(\(gaScroll finished;${interrupted ? \(dq \(dq : \(dq not \(dq}interrupted\(ga);
  \f[B]if\f[R] (interrupted) {
    alert(\(dqScroll interrupted!\(dq);
  }
}
.EE
.PP
When the button is clicked, we immediately apply the
\f[CR]fade\-out\f[R] class to the toolbar, causing it to fade out.
We then run \f[CR]scrollTo(0, 0)\f[R] on the \f[CR]<section>\f[R] to
scroll its content to the top, awaiting its promise resolution as we do
so and storing the \f[CR]result\f[R] in a constant.
When the promise has resolved, we call \f[CR]isInterrupted()\f[R] to
report that the scroll operation has finished and whether it was
interrupted.
Finally, we apply the \f[CR]fade\-in\f[R] class to the toolbar, causing
it to fade back in again.
.IP
.EX
scrollToBtn.addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, \f[B]async\f[R] () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  toolbar.className = \(dqfade\-out\(dq;
  \f[B]const\f[R] result = \f[B]await\f[R] section.scrollTo(0, 0);
  isInterrupted(result.interrupted);
  toolbar.className = \(dqfade\-in\(dq;
});
.EE
.PP
The code not relevant to \f[CR]scrollTo()\f[R] is not shown, for
brevity.
.SS Result
Click the buttons to see the scrolling behavior.
Note how the toolbar fades out when a button is pressed, and fades in
again once the smooth scrolling is finished.
Also try pressing one button and then quickly pressing another button
before the first scrolling operation has finished.
Note how, in these cases, the scrolling is reported as interrupted.
.PP
You can also \c
.UR https://mdn.github.io/dom-examples/scroll-promises/element-methods/
load the demo in a separate tab
.UE \c
\ and view the \c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/dom-examples/tree/main/scroll-promises/element-methods
source code
.UE \c
\&.
.SS Aside on feature detection
If you run this example in a browser that doesn\(cqt support
promise\-returning scroll operations, the scroll operations are still
smooth, but the toolbar doesn\(cqt fade out and then fade back in once
the operation is finished.
The feature detection is handled by a function called
\f[CR]supportsScrollPromises()\f[R], which runs a scroll operation and
tests whether its return value is a promise:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]function\f[R] supportsScrollPromises() {
  \f[B]const\f[R] test = section.scroll(0, 0);
  \f[B]return\f[R] test \f[B]instanceof\f[R] Promise;
}
.EE
.PP
Check out the \c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/dom-examples/blob/main/scroll-promises/element-methods/index.js
source code
.UE \c
\ to see how the feature detection is used.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Element.scrollTop\f[R], \f[CR]Element.scrollLeft\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Window.scrollTo()\f[R]
